Parallel execution and LAPACK
=============================

Load LAPACK during serial execution. Loading through LAPACK.MAC is refused
inside CALL-IN-PARALLEL before initialization; LOAD-LAPACK.LISP also protects
direct Lisp loading. The public entry preserves the operation-named Maxima
error, which LOADFILE would otherwise replace with a generic load failure.

DGEEV, DGESVD, ZGEEV, ZHEEV and DGEQRF require serial execution. Their translated
call paths reach shared initialization in DLAMCH, which publishes FIRST before
DLAMC2 has filled EPS. A concurrent first call can read zero instead of the
machine precision. DGEQRF reaches it through DGEQR2 and DLARFG. The handwritten
adapters refuse before matrix conversion; generated code and serial arithmetic
are unchanged. The same rule applies to native workers, caller participation
and the one-runner fallback, including PARALLEL_THREADS = 1.

The present DGESV/DGETF2 path does not call DLAMCH. DGESV, DGEMM, DLANGE and
ZLANGE remain available after serial package loading. This does not certify
arbitrary surrounding evaluator state, unmarked foreign Lisp threads or
asynchronous interruption as safe.

Tests append to RTEST_DGEQRF.MAC, preserving previous problem numbers:

  run_testsuite(share_tests=true,
                tests=["rtest_dgemm", "rtest_dgeqrf", "rtest_dgesv"]);

On SBCL those files are excluded from the default registry because package
compilation needs considerable heap. Start a fresh process with
MAXIMA_LISP_OPTIONS="--dynamic-space-size 4096", then first register them:

  share_testsuite_files:append(share_testsuite_files,
                              ["rtest_dgemm", "rtest_dgeqrf", "rtest_dgesv"])$

Always use --no-init and a process timeout. A Lisp error can exit zero; require
both completion of all 50 examples and the "No unexpected errors" summary.

The helper LAPACK-PARALLEL-CHECK.LISP uses PARALLEL-INPUT-CHECK.LISP to force
execution sites. Converter and loader hooks stop the unguarded negative
control before translated code or compilation, and are restored after worker
joins. Tests assert the operation-named error and that the body was not entered,
including when the independent input flag is cleared. Both public and direct
Lisp loader paths are covered.

Serial reference tests check 24 generated samples, all optional vector flags,
known real and complex spectra, eigenvector residuals and normalization, QR
reconstruction and orthogonality, and SVD reconstruction, ordering and the
Frobenius norm identity. Zero, singleton, wide and tall matrices exercise
boundaries without prescribing a basis for repeated eigenvalues. References
use small independent Common Lisp matrix calculations and a fixed local LCG;
no LAPACK routine computes its own expected answer. Input matrices must remain
unchanged. Native/fallback batches also check 32 exact LU solutions, products
and real/complex norms. No previous expected mathematical value is weakened.
